[about]
Title=02 - User Interface Concepts
Intro=What a Homer program is made of, each part named by the narrator and then shown by the screen reader: the window, the main view, the dialog, the Say keys, messages, the pick list, menus, and help in four places.
Setup=Nothing is needed; this walk is listened to.
Homework=Open any Homer program and, as each part takes focus, listen for its name, its kind, its value or state, and the hint after a pause.

[step]

Say=A Homer program is heard before it is seen. Each part I name, the reader then shows, as it would on your machine: the name, the kind of control, its value and state, and, a moment later, a hint.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=First, the window. One program, one copy. Its desktop key opens it or brings it forward. Alt plus Control plus D, D for DbDo.
Key=Alt+Control+D
Hear=DbDo
Hear=DbDo ready

[step]

Say=The title as the window came forward, then the program's own greeting, in the reader's message voice. I press the same key again.
Key=Alt+Control+D
Hear=DbDo

[step]

Say=The same window, never a second copy. Next, the main view: a control the reader already knows. In EdSharp, it is an edit box.
Key=Alt+Control+E
Hear=EdSharp
Hear=Untitled, edit, multiline, blank
Hear=Type in text.

[step]

Say=Name: Untitled. Role: edit, multiline. Value: blank, since nothing is typed yet. Then the hint, which tells a newcomer what the control expects. In FileDir, the main view is a list.
Key=Alt+Control+F
Hear=FileDir
Hear=ReadMe dot m d, 1 of 24
Note=Confirm this list item's wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=A list item says its name, then where it sits: 1 of 24. That count is your map. Now a dialog. Every Homer dialog is built the same way, a label, then the control it names. Here is DbDo's search dialog.
Key=
Hear=Keywords dialog
Hear=Keywords: edit
Hear=Type in text.

[step]

Say=The title first, then the label read before its box, then the hint. Tab moves through the controls in the order they were added. At the end of every dialog come OK and Cancel.
Key=Tab
Hear=OK button
Hear=To activate press Spacebar.
Note=Confirm what Tab reaches first with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=OK has no underlined letter: Enter presses it, Escape is Cancel, and Control plus Enter submits from any control in the dialog. Alt plus an underlined letter jumps straight to a control.
Key=Alt+K
Hear=Keywords: edit
Hear=Type in text.
Note=Confirm that K is the label's underlined letter with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Alt plus K, K for Keywords, and focus is back on the box. I press Escape.
Key=Escape
Hear=DbDo
Note=Confirm the line read when the dialog closes with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Back in the main window. Next, the Say keys. They ask a question and change nothing. Shift plus Z is Say Status: Z, the end of the alphabet, where the status line sits.
Key=Shift+Z
Hear=jobs, 12 records, no filter, sorted by employer

[step]

Say=The table, its record count, the filter and the sort, in one breath. Pressed twice quickly, the same words open in a window to arrow through. Next, messages: they are spoken when something happens. In EdSharp, Control plus K sets a bookmark, K for marK.
Key=Control+K
Hear=Bookmark at percent 40

[step]

Say=The fact, with its number, in the message voice, and the same words on the status line. Next, the pick list. Where a field has fixed values, F4 opens them.
Key=F4
Hear=status: list box, untried, 4 of 5
Hear=To move through items press Up or Down Arrow.

[step]

Say=Name: status. Role: list box. Value: untried. Position: 4 of 5. Then the hint. A letter jumps to a value, so nothing is typed that could be mistyped. Next, menus, which say their own keys. F10 opens the menu bar.
Key=F10
Hear=Menu bar
Hear=File menu

[step]

Say=I arrow down to the first item.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=Open Database..., Control plus O, O

[step]

Say=The item, its hotkey, then its letter. Learn the key from the menu once, and skip the menu after that. I leave the menus.
Key=Escape
Hear=Leaving menus

[step]

Say=Help is in four places, the same in every Homer program. I say which; the reader says the key.
Key=
Hear=F1, the guide

[step]

Say=The changes, release by release.
Key=
Hear=Shift plus F1, History

[step]

Say=The version, and a check for a newer one.
Key=
Hear=Alt plus F1, About

[step]

Say=The name of any key you press.
Key=
Hear=Control plus F1, Key Describer

[step]

Say=The Help menu holds all four, and Play Tutorials. Two more parts work out of sight. Your data folder holds your own copies of the program's templates, and an update never touches them. The logs folder holds one log per session, which is what a problem report attaches.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A planned misstep. In the main window, Shift plus Z asks a question. In an edit box, the same keys type a capital Z, so a Say key is for the window, not the field. I press it in EdSharp's edit box.
Key=Shift+Z
Hear=Z
Note=Confirm how the reader echoes a typed capital with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=The reader echoed the letter, and the box now holds a Z. Control plus Z undoes it.
Key=Control+Z
Hear=Undo
Note=Confirm the undo echo with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I name the part; the reader says the key or the word.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The desktop key for DbDo.
Key=
Hear=Alt plus Control plus D

[step]

Say=The class every Homer dialog is built with.
Key=
Hear=Lbc

[step]

Say=The question key that changes nothing.
Key=
Hear=Shift plus Z, Say Status

[step]

Say=The list of a field's values.
Key=
Hear=F4, pick list

[step]

Say=The menu bar.
Key=
Hear=F10

[step]

Say=The guide.
Key=
Hear=F1
